Let $A$ be a Banach algebra. A multi-norm based on the underlying Banach space $A$ is a sequence $(\|\cdot\|_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$, where $\|\cdot\|_n$ is a norm on the $n$-fold Cartesian product $A^n$, satisfying the standard multi-norm axioms:
(i) $\|\cdot\|_1 = \|\cdot\|$ on $A$;
(ii) for every permutation $\sigma$ of $\{1,\dots,n\}$, $\|(a_{\sigma(1)},\dots,a_{\sigma(n)})\|_n = \|(a_1,\dots,a_n)\|_n$;
(iii) for scalars $\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_n$, $\|(\alpha_1 a_1,\dots,\alpha_n a_n)\|_n \le (\max_i |\alpha_i|)\,\|(a_1,\dots,a_n)\|_n$;
(iv) $\|(a_1,\dots,a_{n-1},0)\|_n = \|(a_1,\dots,a_{n-1})\|_{n-1}$;
(v) $\|(a_1,\dots,a_{n-1},a_{n-1})\|_n = \|(a_1,\dots,a_{n-1})\|_{n-1}$.

Assume additionally the submultiplicativity condition
$$\|(a_1 b_1, \dots, a_n b_n)\|_n \;\le\; \|(a_1,\dots,a_n)\|_n\,\|(b_1,\dots,b_n)\|_n$$
for every $n\in\mathbb{N}$ and all $a_i,b_i\in A$. Call $(A,(\|\cdot\|_n))$ a multi-Banach algebra.

Develop a useful general theory of multi-Banach algebras: in particular, identify natural classes of examples, intrinsic structural results (analogues of ideals, representations, spectrum, characters, automatic continuity), and connections to classical Banach algebra theory.

